====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal|chiefly|Britain|Ireland|Commonwealth|dated|_|in the|_|US}} A [[male]] person.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:man}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1922|author=w:P. G. Wodehouse|title=Right Ho, Jeeves|chapter=19|passage=You are a silly young '''geezer'''.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=2023|writer=w:Nathan Bryon; Tom Melia|director=w:Raine Allen-Miller|actor=w:Simon Manyonda|role=Nathan|title=w:Rye Lane|text=See the hoverboard-riding '''geezer'''? This close to payin' a G for a shot of my cousin Calvin's molars.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|Ireland|Commonwealth|chiefly|Cockney|slang}} Someone [[affable]] but [[morally]] [[dubious]]; a [[wide boy]].
#: {{syn|en|spiv|wide boy}}
#* {{quote-song
|en
|title=Geezers need excitement
|album=Original Pirate Material
|artist=The Streets
|year=2002
|passage='''Geezers''' need excitement / If their lives don't provide 'em this, they incite violence / Common sense, simple common sense}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Carlton Leach|title=Muscle|publisher=John Blake Publishing|isbn=9781784184728
|passage=He turned out to be a proper '''geezer''' who was willing to listen to my proposition that if he took the door at the Ministry, I would pay him £400 a month to mark my cards.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Dreda Say Mitchell|title=Geezer Girls|publisher=Hachette UK|isbn=9781848946163
|passage=He was a bit of a '''geezer'''. Used to box with the Krays when he was a young &#39;un.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Charlotte Ward|title=Why Am I Always the One Before 'The One'?|publisher=Hachette UK|isbn=9780755364800
|passage=When I&#39;d first met Adam, at work when we were both 23, the fact that he seemed a little rough around the edges appealed to me. He was a bit of a '''geezer''', a joker, one of the lads.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|Ireland|Commonwealth|slang}} {{n-g|Term of address for a [[male]].}}
#: {{syn|en|mate|Thesaurus:friend}}
#: {{ux|en|Hi, '''geezer''', you alright?}}
# {{lb|en|informal|chiefly|North America|sometimes|mildly|derogatory}} An [[old]] person, usually a male, typically a cranky old man.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:old man}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1885|author=Corin|title=The Truth about the Stage|passage=In the right-hand division lay the two old '''geezers''', as Sandy styled the landlord and his wife.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=25 Aug 2000|author=Moira McDonald|titleurl=http://community.seattletimes.nwsource.com/archive/?date=20000825&slug=4038797|title=Outtakes|journal=Seattle Times|accessdate=6 Sep 2008
|passage=The technical term for a female '''geezer''' is "old broad," but this is irrelevant, as nobody in Hollywood makes films about women over 55.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=The Geezer Gallery|title=|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140428111606/http://www.geezergallery.com/content/why-geezer|accessdate=31 Jan 2014
|passage=Why '''Geezer'''? Why would a fine arts gallery choose a name that conjures images of a grumpy old guy sitting on the front porch hollering, “get off my lawn”?}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|Britain|Ireland|Commonwealth|slang}} [[wife|Wife]]; [[old]] [[woman]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:wife|Thesaurus:old woman}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1882|author=J. F. Mitchell|title=Jimmy Johnson's Holiday|passage=He'd flirt and boat, but never wrote / A note to his old '''geezer'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1886|title=Her Mother's Got the Hump
|passage=This frizzle-headed old '''geezer''' had a chin on her as rough well, as rough as her family, and they're rough 'uns.}}

